.TH _PRINTF 3 "April 2026" "Holberton School" "Custom Library Functions"

.SH NAME
_printf \- formatted output conversion

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "main.h"
.PP
.BI "int _printf(const char *" format ", ...);"

.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B _printf()
function produces output according to a
.I format
string. It writes output to the standard output stream
.BR stdout .
.PP
The format string is composed of zero or more directives: ordinary characters
(not
.BR % ),
which are copied unchanged to the output stream, and conversion
specifications, each of which fetches zero or more subsequent arguments.
.PP
Each conversion specification is introduced by the character
.BR % .
The arguments must correspond properly (after type promotion) with the
conversion specifier.

.SS Conversion Specifiers
The following conversion specifiers are supported:
.TP 8
.B %c
The
.I int
argument is converted to an
.IR "unsigned char" ,
and the resulting character is written.
.TP
.B %s
The
.I "const char *"
argument is expected to be a pointer to a character array (string).
Characters from the array are written up to (but not including) a
terminating null byte. If the pointer is
.BR NULL ,
the string
.B (null)
is printed instead.
.TP
.B %%
A literal
.B %
is written. No argument is consumed.

.SH RETURN VALUE
Upon successful return,
.B _printf()
returns the number of characters printed (excluding the null byte used
to end output to strings).
.PP
If
.I format
is
.BR NULL ,
the function returns
.BR \-1 .

.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Print a simple string
.nf
_printf("Hello, World!\\n");
.fi
.PP
Output:
.B Hello, World!

.SS Print a character
.nf
_printf("Letter: %c\\n", 'A');
.fi
.PP
Output:
.B Letter: A

.SS Print a string variable
.nf
_printf("Name: %s\\n", "Holberton");
.fi
.PP
Output:
.B Name: Holberton

.SS Print a literal percent sign
.nf
_printf("100%%\\n");
.fi
.PP
Output:
.B 100%

.SS Handle NULL string
.nf
_printf("Value: %s\\n", NULL);
.fi
.PP
Output:
.B Value: (null)

.SS Unknown specifier
.nf
_printf("%r\\n");
.fi
.PP
Output:
.B %r

.SH ERRORS
.B _printf()
returns \-1 in the following cases:
.IP \(bu 3
The
.I format
string is
.BR NULL .
.IP \(bu
A
.B %
character is the last character in the format string (incomplete specifier).

.SH CONFORMING TO
This function is a simplified implementation of
.BR printf (3)
from the C standard library. It does not support field widths, precision,
length modifiers, flags, or numeric conversion specifiers such as
.BR %d ", " %i ", " %u ", " %o ", " %x ", or " %X .

